  schema={schema}
  ogType="website"
>

<PageHero
    eyebrow="Technical SEO"
    crumbs={[{"label": "Services", "href": "/services/"}, {"label": "Technical SEO"}]}
    lead="Technical SEO fixes the parts of your site that stop Google crawling, indexing and ranking your pages. I audit it, rank the fixes by impact and carry them out."
    cta={{"href": "/free-seo-audit-guildford/", "label": "Get my free audit"}}
    glance={{"heading": "At a glance", "points": ["I crawl your site and list what stops Google finding, rendering and indexing your pages.", "Fixes are ranked by effort and likely impact. The quick wins come first.", "Typical areas: speed and Core Web Vitals, indexation, redirects, structured data and mobile.", "Ranking changes usually show within four to eight weeks of the fixes going live."], "facts": [{"k": "One-off technical audit", "v": "£500"}, {"k": "Ongoing work", "v": "From £500 a month"}, {"k": "Free first look", "v": "Free SEO audit, 2 to 3 working days"}, {"k": "Who does the work", "v": "Me, directly"}]}}
    proof={[{"v": "£500", "l": "one-off technical audit. Monthly plans start at £500.", "href": "/seo-pricing-guildford/"}, {"v": "2 to 3 working days", "l": "for the free first look at your site.", "href": "/free-seo-audit-guildford/"}, {"v": "3,510", "l": "Google clicks in October 2026 on my own EV charging site. May was 188.", "href": "/case-studies/"}]}
  >
    <Fragment slot="title">Technical SEO for <span class="mark">Guildford</span> Businesses</Fragment>
  </PageHero>

  <article class="doc">
  <div class="prose">

    <div class="reveal">
    <h2>What is technical SEO</h2>

    <p>Technical SEO decides whether Google can crawl, render, index and rank your pages. It has six parts.</p>

    <dl class="def-grid not-prose">
  <div><dt>Site speed</dt><dd>Server response and page load speed, measured against Core Web Vitals thresholds.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Crawlability</dt><dd>Crawl directives and rendering, so Googlebot can reach every page that matters.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Indexation</dt><dd>Controls that decide which pages Google adds to its index.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Structured data</dt><dd>Markup that tells search engines what each page offers.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Site architecture</dt><dd>URL structure and internal links that guide crawlers and visitors.</dd></div>
  <div><dt>Mobile performance</dt><dd>Google indexes the mobile version of your site rather than desktop.</dd></div>
</dl>

    <p>Good copy and strong links earn little on a site that Google cannot crawl or that loads slowly. I fix this layer first.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
    <h2>Why Guildford businesses need technical SEO</h2>

    <p>The technical problems depend on how the site is built and what it holds. These are the ones I see most.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>JavaScript-heavy sites</strong> built on React, Angular or Vue need server-side rendering or pre-rendering so Google can index the content.</li>
      <li><strong>Property sites</strong> have thousands of listing pages. That causes crawl budget pressure, duplicate content and index bloat from expired listings.</li>
      <li><strong>Professional services sites</strong> compete for high-value local searches. Page speed and mobile performance affect conversion.</li>
      <li><strong>Media-heavy sites</strong> carry large images and video that slow pages without optimisation.</li>
      <li><strong>Healthcare sites</strong> are YMYL. Technical trust signals carry extra weight.</li>
    </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
    <h2>Core Web Vitals and site speed</h2>

    <p>Core Web Vitals are Google's three measures of loading, interactivity and visual stability.</p>

    <h3>Loading speed (LCP)</h3>

    <p>Google wants Largest Contentful Paint under 2.5 seconds. Most failing sites I audit have unoptimised hero images, render-blocking CSS or slow shared hosting. I fix it with WebP and AVIF images, preloaded critical assets, fewer blocking resources and better hosting where needed.</p>

    <h3>Responsiveness (INP)</h3>

    <p>Interaction to Next Paint replaced First Input Delay in March 2024. It measures every interaction and not just the first. Long scripts that block the main thread are the usual cause. I fix it with code splitting, deferred scripts, web worker offloading and fewer third-party scripts.</p>

    <h3>Visual stability (CLS)</h3>

    <p>Cumulative Layout Shift measures elements that jump during load. Common causes are images without dimensions, injected ad units and web fonts. I set explicit dimensions, reserve space for dynamic content and use font-display settings.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
    <h2>Crawlability and indexation</h2>

    <p>If Google cannot find your pages nothing else matters. I check four things.</p>

    <h3>Robots.txt and crawl directives</h3>

    <p>A bad directive can block whole sections of your site. I audit robots.txt, meta robots tags and X-Robots-Tag headers together.</p>

    <h3>XML sitemaps</h3>

    <p>A sitemap should list only indexable canonical URLs. I build dynamic sitemaps that exclude noindex pages, redirects and pagination parameters.</p>

    <h3>Crawl budget</h3>

    <p>Large sites waste crawl budget on faceted navigation, parameters, tag archives and expired content. I use noindex directives, canonical tags, Search Console parameter handling and internal links that favour your key pages.</p>

    <h3>Indexation monitoring</h3>

    <p>I watch the Search Console Index Coverage report alongside server logs. This finds pages that Google discovers but will not index. The status is "Crawled - currently not indexed".</p>
    </div>

    <div class="not-prose my-12 py-8 border-y border-border">
      <p class="eyebrow mb-3">Quick enquiry</p>
      <p class="font-heading text-brand text-xl mb-4">Not sure which of these issues apply to your site?</p>
      <QuickForm heading="" subject="SEO Guildford: Technical SEO mid-page enquiry" />
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
    <h2>Structured data and schema markup</h2>

    <p>Structured data helps search engines understand your pages and can earn rich results. I add it as inline JSON-LD.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>LocalBusiness schema</strong> with name, address, phone, opening hours, geo-coordinates and service areas.</li>
      <li><strong>Service schema</strong> that links your offers to your business.</li>
      <li><strong>FAQPage schema</strong> for accordion results in search.</li>
      <li><strong>BreadcrumbList schema</strong> that shows your site hierarchy in results.</li>
      <li><strong>Review and AggregateRating schema</strong> where you have genuine reviews.</li>
      <li><strong>Event schema</strong> for businesses that host events.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>I validate it in Google's Rich Results Test and monitor it in Search Console.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
    <h2>Mobile-first optimisation</h2>

    <p>Google has indexed the mobile version of your site since 2023. A weak mobile experience lowers your rankings however good the desktop site looks.</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Responsive design</strong> checked across breakpoints.</li>
      <li><strong>Touch targets</strong> big enough to tap without errors.</li>
      <li><strong>Viewport settings</strong> that stop horizontal scroll.</li>
      <li><strong>Mobile page speed</strong>, which is usually slower than desktop.</li>
      <li><strong>Content parity</strong> so mobile shows the same content as desktop. That includes text behind accordions or tabs.</li>
    </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
    <h2>Technical SEO for Surrey Research Park companies</h2>

    <p>Technology sites on Surrey Research Park often need JavaScript rendering, international targeting and stricter security.</p>

    <h3>JavaScript rendering</h3>

    <p>React, Vue, Next.js and Nuxt can leave pages invisible to search engines. I compare rendered and raw HTML in the URL Inspection tool. Then I set up server-side rendering or static generation.</p>

    <h3>International targeting</h3>

    <p>Global audiences need hreflang, international URL structures and geo-targeting. One hreflang error can send the wrong language to the wrong market.</p>

    <h3>Security headers</h3>

    <p>I audit TLS, mixed content and security headers (HSTS, CSP, X-Frame-Options). SEO changes must not conflict with your security rules.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
    <h2>Technical SEO for gaming studios</h2>

    <p>Gaming sites are heavy on media and serve global audiences.</p>

    <h3>Media asset optimisation</h3>

    <p>Trailers and screenshots can push a page past 10MB. I use lazy loading, modern image formats, video facades and a CDN.</p>

    <h3>Launch traffic</h3>

    <p>Launches spike traffic. I set up caching, CDN rules and server scaling so Core Web Vitals hold.</p>

    <h3>Community and forum indexation</h3>

    <p>Forums and user content cause index bloat. I use pagination controls, canonical tags for duplicate threads and noindex for profile pages and empty categories.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
    <h2>How I deliver technical SEO</h2>

    <p>I work in four phases and measure every change.</p>

    <h3>Phase 1: technical audit</h3>

    <p>I crawl the whole site and cross-check Search Console data. I analyse server logs and test Core Web Vitals on each device type. You get a list ranked by impact. See my <a href="/seo-audit-guildford/">SEO audit service</a>.</p>

    <h3>Phase 2: prioritisation and roadmap</h3>

    <p>I rank findings as critical, high, medium or low. You get a roadmap with timelines.</p>

    <h3>Phase 3: implementation</h3>

    <p>I make changes directly where I have CMS or server access. Otherwise I write exact specifications for your developers. Every change is checked before and after. URL changes get full redirect mapping.</p>

    <h3>Phase 4: monitoring</h3>

    <p>Technical SEO has no finish line. Algorithm updates and CMS patches cause quiet regressions. Monitoring catches them while they are small.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
    <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
    </div>

    <div>
      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How long does a technical SEO project take for a Guildford business?</summary>
        <p>Most technical SEO projects run four to twelve weeks depending on site size. A 50-page brochure site for a Guildford professional services firm typically takes four to six weeks, whereas larger sites with thousands of pages, such as property listings or e-commerce catalogues, require eight to twelve weeks for thorough implementation.</p>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What is the difference between technical SEO and on-page SEO?</summary>
        <p>Technical SEO addresses the infrastructure that search engines rely upon to crawl, render, and index your site. On-page SEO focuses on content quality, keyword targeting, and user intent. Both disciplines are necessary: a technically sound site with poor content will not rank, and excellent content on a compromised site will never be properly indexed.</p>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Do you work with technology companies on Surrey Research Park?</summary>
        <p>I do. I have deep experience with the specific technical requirements of gaming and technology companies on Surrey Research Park, including JavaScript-heavy single-page applications, large media assets, and international audience targeting.</p>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>How much does technical SEO cost in Guildford?</summary>
        <p>Technical SEO pricing depends on your site's complexity and current state. A standalone technical SEO audit is a fixed £500, and ongoing technical SEO retainers for Guildford businesses are structured on a monthly basis, with plans starting at £500 per month. I would be pleased to discuss a specific quote based on your requirements.</p>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Will technical SEO improvements affect my current rankings?</summary>
        <p>Technical SEO changes are implemented with considerable care to protect existing rankings. I follow a staged approach. I audit first. Then I prioritise fixes by impact and risk. URL changes and redirects are handled with 301 mappings to preserve link equity. Most sites see ranking improvements within four to eight weeks of implementation.</p>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>Can you address a Google Core Update ranking decline for my Surrey business?</summary>
        <p>Core Updates are not penalties but rather quality re-evaluations. If your Guildford business lost rankings following a Core Update, a technical audit identifies whether the cause is infrastructure-related (speed, crawlability, rendering) or content-related. I address the technical factors directly and recommend content improvements where appropriate.</p>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What is the difference between a 301 and 302 redirect, and why does it matter for SEO?</summary>
        <p>A 301 redirect signals a permanent move and passes ranking value to the new URL. A 302 redirect signals a temporary move and tells search engines the original URL should stay indexed. Using a 302 for a permanent change can leave ranking signals stuck on the old URL. Redirect type matters during migrations, rebrands and URL restructuring.</p>
      </details>

      <details class="faq-item">
        <summary>What does robots.txt do and can it accidentally block my site from Google?</summary>
        <p>A robots.txt file tells search engine crawlers which pages or directories they should and should not access. A single misconfigured line can block an entire site from crawling. An example is a blanket Disallow rule left over from a staging environment. Check robots.txt after every migration or platform change.</p>
      </details>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal">
    <h2>Request a technical SEO audit for your Guildford business</h2>

    <p>Every engagement starts with an audit. It maps what is broken and what is missing. You get a ranked roadmap so the first pound fixes the biggest problem.</p>

    <p>If rankings are weak the cause is often technical. <a href="/free-seo-audit-guildford/">A free SEO audit</a> is the lower-commitment start. Pricing is set out on the pricing page.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 mt-8 not-prose">
      <a href="/seo-audit-guildford/" class="cta-button">Request your audit</a>
      <a href="/services/" class="cta-button-outline">View all services</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Related services</h2>

    <ul>
      <li><a href="/seo-audit-guildford/">SEO audit for Guildford businesses</a>: a site review covering technical, content and local SEO.</li>
      <li><a href="/ai-search-optimisation-guildford/">AI search optimisation</a>: prepare your site for ChatGPT, Perplexity and Google AI Overviews.</li>
      <li><a href="/services/">All SEO services</a>: the full list for Guildford and Surrey businesses.</li>
      <li><a href="/">SEO consultancy for Guildford businesses</a>: the general overview.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</article>

